{"step_index":849,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T07:07:03Z","thinking":"The agent's analysis reveals a critical flaw. The model consistently misclassifies benign inputs, identifying them as severe vulnerabilities like SQL Injection, Command Injection, and Path Traversal. Furthermore, the model hallucinates `CWE-22` in situations involving SSRF, XSS, and similar vulnerabilities.\n\n\nThe overfitting, characterized by a low loss and rote memorization, likely stems from an imbalance in the training data, specifically the limited quantity and repetitive nature of path traversal examples. Adjusting the LoRA hyperparameters will reduce the model capacity and prevent this behavior. A balanced dataset with diverse vulnerability types and benign code examples is essential for generalization.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\k4ran\\\\.gemini\\\\antigravity\\\\brain\\\\e2c1cf9f-2dff-4bf4-a974-ef410441acfa\\\\implementation_plan.md\"","toolAction":"\"Viewing existing implementation plan\"","toolSummary":"\"View implementation_plan.md\""}}]}
{"step_index":850,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T07:07:04Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-22T12:37:04+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-22T12:37:05+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/k4ran/.gemini/antigravity/brain/e2c1cf9f-2dff-4bf4-a974-ef410441acfa/implementation_plan.md`\nTotal Lines: 276\nTotal Bytes: 9585\nShowing lines 1 to 276\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # 🔥 Fluxnat Coder 3B — Complete Fine-Tuning & Rebranding Plan\n2: \n3: > Transform `Qwen 2.5 Coder 3B Abliterated` into your own **Fluxnat Coder 3B** — a fully rebranded, cybersecurity-specialized coding model.\n4: \n5: ---\n6: \n7: ## What We're Building\n8: \n9: | | |\n10: |---|---|\n11: | **Base Model** | `huihui-ai/Qwen2.5-Coder-3B-Instruct-abliterated` |\n12: | **Output Model** | `fluxnat/Fluxnat-Coder-3B` |\n13: | **Training Framework** | **Unsloth** (2x faster, 70% less VRAM) |\n14: | **Fine-Tuning Method** | QLoRA (4-bit) → Merge → Full model |\n15: | **Datasets** | CyberSecurity-1M + CyberLLMInstruct + CTI + custom |\n16: | **Training Hardware** | Free Google Colab T4 (16GB) or local GPU |\n17: | **Training Time** | ~4–8 hours on Colab T4 |\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## Architecture: 3-Stage Training Pipeline\n22: \n23: ```mermaid\n24: flowchart LR\n25:     A[\"🏗️ Base Model\\nQwen 2.5 Coder 3B\\nAbliterated\"] --> B[\"Stage 1\\nDomain Pre-training\\nCyberSecurity-1M\"]\n26:     B --> C[\"Stage 2\\nInstruction Tuning\\nCyberLLMInstruct\\n+ CTI Dataset\"]\n27:     C --> D[\"Stage 3\\nCoT Reasoning\\nDistillation\"]\n28:     D --> E[\"🔥 Fluxnat Coder 3B\\nFully Rebranded\"]\n29:     E --> F[\"📦 Publish\\nHuggingFace Hub\"]\n30: ```\n31: \n32: ### Why 3 Stages?\n33: | Stage | Purpose | Dataset | What It Teaches |\n34: |-------|---------|---------|-----------------|\n35: | **Stage 1** | Domain knowledge injection | CyberSecurity-1M (~1M samples) | CVEs, threat intel, security concepts, attack patterns |\n36: | **Stage 2** | Task-specific instruction\n<truncated 7344 bytes>\n Questions\n228: \n229: > [!WARNING]\n230: > 1. **Do you have a HuggingFace account?** Needed to download datasets and publish `fluxnat/Fluxnat-Coder-3B`.\n231: > 2. **Where will you train?** Google Colab (free), local GPU, or cloud rental?\n232: > 3. **Custom training data?** Do you have any of your own vulnerable code samples you'd like to include?\n233: > 4. **HuggingFace org name?** Should it be published as `fluxnat/Fluxnat-Coder-3B` or a different username?\n234: \n235: ---\n236: \n237: ## Build Order\n238: \n239: ```mermaid\n240: flowchart LR\n241:     S1[\"Step 1\\nprepare_datasets.py\\n📦 Download & format\"] --> S2[\"Step 2\\ntrain.py\\n🏋️ 3-stage training\"]\n242:     S2 --> S3[\"Step 3\\nmerge_and_export.py\\n🔗 Merge LoRA → full\"]\n243:     S3 --> S4[\"Step 4\\nrebrand.py\\n🎨 Rename to Fluxnat\"]\n244:     S4 --> S5[\"Step 5\\nevaluate.py\\n📊 Benchmark\"]\n245:     S5 --> S6[\"Step 6\\n🚀 Publish to\\nHuggingFace Hub\"]\n246: ```\n247: \n248: ---\n249: \n250: ## What Fluxnat Coder 3B Will Be Able To Do\n251: \n252: ```\n253: 🔍 \"Analyze this Python function for security vulnerabilities\"\n254:    → Identifies SQL injection, XSS, path traversal, etc.\n255:    → Explains the vulnerability chain step-by-step\n256:    → Provides a secure fix with code\n257: \n258: 🛡️ \"What CWE does this vulnerability fall under?\"\n259:    → Maps to CWE-89, CWE-79, CWE-22, etc.\n260:    → Explains OWASP Top 10 category\n261: \n262: ⚡ \"Generate a proof-of-concept exploit for this bug\"\n263:    → Creates PoC payloads (uncensored — no refusals)\n264: \n265: 🔧 \"Rewrite this code to be secure\"\n266:    → Produces secure alternative with explanation\n267: \n268: 📊 \"Rate the severity of this vulnerability\"\n269:    → CVSS-like scoring with detailed rationale\n270: ```\n271: \n272: ---\n273: \n274: > [!IMPORTANT]\n275: > **Approve this plan and I'll start building all the scripts!** I'll create every file in the `fluxnat-coder-3b/` directory inside your project.\n276: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":851,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T07:07:11Z","thinking":"Examining the implementation plan, the task involves removing specific model identifiers. This necessitates a search-and-replace operation across the document. The primary concern is ensuring complete removal and verifying no accidental re-introductions occur during editing.\n\nThe current strategy involves using a find-and-replace method. A thorough review will follow to confirm all instances are addressed and that the document's structure remains sound.\n\n\nAdjusted LoRA rank and alpha, reduced training epochs and learning rate to mitigate potential overfitting on the smaller dataset, and introduced a warmup ratio. Expanded the dataset with additional CWE types, but critically added benign code examples to improve balance and provide counterfactual training data. Need to also verify that the `implementation_plan.md` adheres to the required schema, including user review, proposed changes, verification plan, and feedback flags.\n\n\nEnsured the benign code examples cover safe patterns. The dataset preparation script now balances vulnerable, benign, and identity alignment records. Verified the training configurations for LoRA, epochs, learning rate, and warmup steps, and integrated dataset generation into the notebook.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":"{\"RequestFeedback\":true,\"Summary\":\"Implementation plan to resolve overfitting in Cell 6 (bBtUZIG4wjco) of the training notebook. Calibrates LoRA hyperparameters (rank 16, alpha 32, 2 epochs, lr 1e-4), introduces balanced negative benign samples alongside diverse CWE vulnerability samples to eliminate false-positive bias, and updates local scripts and notebook cells.\",\"UserFacing\":true}","CodeContent":"\"# Calibration Plan: Resolve Overfitting in Cell 6 (`bBtUZIG4wjco`) & Eliminate False Positives\\n\\n## Problem Diagnosis & Root Cause\\nDuring the initial Colab training run of **Fluxnat Coder 3B**, training completed 60 steps over 5 epochs on 90 samples, driving loss down to **0.0032**. \\n\\n### Empirical Symptoms\\n1. **Severe Overfitting / Memorization:** Loss of 0.0032 indicates near-zero cross-entropy, causing the model to memorize exact token sequences rather than learning generalizable security semantics.\\n2. **False Positive Rate = 100% (Resistance = 0.0%):** Because the initial dataset contained **0 benign samples**, the model learned an inductive bias that *all* input code is a Critical CVSS 9.8 vulnerability. Safe patterns (parameterized queries, array subprocesses, path sanitization) were flagged as critical vulnerabilities.\\n3. **CWE-22 Collapse:** The model defaulted to classifying unrelated bugs (SSRF, XSS, deserialization, secrets) as Path Traversal (CWE-22) because CWE-22 was heavily over-represented and memorized.\\n4. **LoRA Capacity Bloat:** LoRA rank $r=64$ ($\\\\alpha=128$) allocated 119.7M trainable parameters, which is dramatically oversized for a small seed dataset.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## User Review Required\\n> [!IMPORTANT]\\n> - **Colab Cell Execution:** We will update `Fluxnat_Coder_3B_Training.ipynb` and provide the exact cell code replacements for Cells 3, 4, and 5 (Cell `bBtUZIG4wjco`).\\n> - **Training Time:** The recalibrated run will take **~75–90 seconds** on Colab T4 (25 steps instead of 60).\\n> - **Target Loss Range:** The calibrated loss will settle between **0.60 and 0.85**. This preserves model entropy, general coding ability, and semantic reasoning while instilling security auditing expertise and model identity.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Proposed Changes\\n\\n### 1. Training Configuration & Hyperparameter Optimization\\n#### [MODIFY] [`config/training_config.yaml`](file:///c:/Users/k4ran/OneDrive/Desktop/smartAGENT/config/training_config.yaml)\\n- Reduce LoRA rank $r$ from 64 to 16, and alpha from 128 to 3\n<truncated 2599 bytes>","Description":"\"Implementation plan to resolve Cell 6 overfitting and calibrate training parameters\"","Overwrite":"true","TargetFile":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\k4ran\\\\.gemini\\\\antigravity\\\\brain\\\\e2c1cf9f-2dff-4bf4-a974-ef410441acfa\\\\implementation_plan.md\"","toolAction":"\"Writing implementation plan\"","toolSummary":"\"Write implementation_plan.md\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
